WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.770
Half the class submits a recursive

2
00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:05.450
factorial that crashes with a stack

3
00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:06.010
overflow.

4
00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:09.610
Their loop version worked perfectly.

5
00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.800
What do they not yet understand?

6
00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:17.490
Today: how recursion really executes,

7
00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:20.710
why induction proves it correct, and

8
00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:22.870
the mental models that stop students

9
00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:24.010
treating it as magic.

10
00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:27.840
A recursive function solves a problem by

11
00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.180
calling itself on a smaller instance.

12
00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:32.360
It needs a base case that

13
00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:33.220
answers directly.

14
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:36.040
And every call must move

15
00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:37.800
closer to that base case.

16
00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:41.620
Each call gets its own frame with its

17
00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:43.860
own parameters and local variables.

18
00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:46.760
Frames pile up until the

19
00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:47.960
base case returns.

20
00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:52.040
Then they unwind one by one, each

21
00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:53.560
finishing its own multiplication.

22
00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:57.610
The crashing version tested for n equal

23
00:00:57.610 --> 00:00:59.950
to one but was called with zero.

24
00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:03.370
Zero minus one never reaches one,

25
00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:05.330
so the frames never stop.

26
00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:09.450
Testing for n at most zero fixes it.

27
00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:13.070
Structural induction mirrors the code.

28
00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:14.860
Prove the base case

29
00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:16.240
returns the right answer.

30
00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:19.530
Then assume the smaller call is correct

31
00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:21.670
and show the current call is too.

32
00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:23.890
That is exactly the

33
00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:25.530
recursive leap of faith.

34
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.100
Students often picture recursion as

35
00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:31.300
a loop, or believe the function

36
00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:33.320
somehow waits without memory.

37
00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:36.970
Act it out: each student is one frame,

38
00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:39.110
holding a card with their own n.

39
00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:42.020
Then trace on paper before

40
00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:42.960
running any code.

41
00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:44.800
Quick check!

42
00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:49.650
The fixed factorial is called with four.

43
00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:52.710
How many frames are on the

44
00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:54.890
stack at the deepest point?

45
00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:59.520
Five: fact of four, three,

46
00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:02.800
two, one and zero.

47
00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:06.430
Each holds its own n, waiting for

48
00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:08.130
the call below it to return.

49
00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:11.800
In the app, open the recursion lab.

50
00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:14.330
Step through the calls and watch

51
00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:17.050
frames push and pop on the stack.

52
00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:19.080
Your turn.

53
00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:21.780
In the induction proof for a

54
00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:23.740
recursive function, what do you

55
00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:25.380
assume in the inductive step?

56
00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.860
You assume the smaller recursive

57
00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:30.600
call returns the correct answer.

58
00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:33.790
Remember: every recursion needs a

59
00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:36.170
reachable base case, frames hold

60
00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:38.650
separate state, and induction is the

61
00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:38.890
proof.

62
00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:42.430
Try it in your next lecture: line

63
00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:44.550
up five students as stack frames

64
00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:46.070
for factorial of four.

65
00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:49.160
Each writes their n on a card and

66
00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:50.680
passes the result back up.

67
00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:52.460
Then the class writes the

68
00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:53.640
induction proof together.
